==English==

===Etymology===
From the way a cat, when angry, raises or arches its back.

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} One is offended or angry.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1835 |title=The Life and Letters of William Cowper  |author=William Hayley  |page= |ISBN= |passage='''My back is up''', and I cannot hear the thought of wooing him any farther, nor would do it, though he were as pig a gentleman (look you) as Lucifer himself. }}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2011 |title=The Baby Truce  |author=Jeannie Watt  |page=84 |ISBN=0373717490 |passage=And I want you to be nice to Patty. For some reason '''her back is up'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=2014|title=Cabin Girl |author=Kristin Butcher  |page= |ISBN=1459806522 |passage=Now '''my back is up'''. “Yes, the witch,” I hiss. }}

